  "steps": [
    {
      "id": "precipitate",
      "name": "Precipitate the group",
      "tube": "a",
      "reagentIds": [
        "hcl-6m"
      ],
      "ruleIds": [
        "chlorides-form"
      ],
      "substanceIds": [
        "silver-chloride",
        "lead-chloride",
        "mercury-i-chloride"
      ],
      "equilibriumIds": [
        "silver-chloride",
        "lead-chloride",
        "mercury-i-chloride"
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "value": "Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s)",
          "sourceId": "birk-silver",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        },
        {
          "value": "Pb²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) → PbCl₂(s)",
          "sourceId": "birk-lead",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        },
        {
          "value": "Hg₂²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq) → Hg₂Cl₂(s)",
          "sourceId": "birk-mercury",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "value": "The white solid is the chloride of each of the three ions that the sample holds: silver chloride, lead chloride, mercury(I) chloride. The chlorides of almost every other metal dissolve, so one reagent takes these three out of a mixture together. That is why they are called Group I.",
          "sourceId": "malik-group-1-separation",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        },
        {
          "value": "Lead chloride is the most soluble of the three, and it is slow to form. On a real bench some lead stays in the liquid, and the acid goes in a drop at a time until no more solid comes. Far too much acid begins to dissolve the chlorides again. This lab adds a fixed amount and takes all three to come down whole.",
          "sourceId": "birk-lead",
          "alsoSourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab",
          "moreSourceIds": [
            "malik-group-1-separation"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "extract-lead",
      "name": "Take the lead out",
      "tube": "a",
      "reagentIds": [
        "water"
      ],
      "ruleIds": [
        "lead-chloride-in-water"
      ],
      "substanceIds": [
        "lead-chloride",
        "silver-chloride",
        "mercury-i-chloride",
        "lead-ion"
      ],
      "equilibriumIds": [],
      "equations": [
        {
          "value": "PbCl₂(s) → Pb²⁺(aq) + 2Cl⁻(aq)",
          "sourceId": "smc-group-1-lab",
          "alsoSourceId": "malik-group-1-ions"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "value": "Hot water holds about three times as much lead chloride as cold water. Silver chloride and mercury(I) chloride hardly dissolve in either. So the hot liquid carries the lead away, and the other two stay behind as the solid.",
          "sourceId": "birk-lead",
          "alsoSourceId": "malik-group-1-ions"
        },
        {
          "value": "If the water is poured off cold, part of the lead chloride is still with the solid, and it is still there at step 4. A real bench goes further than this lab does: it washes the solid with hot water two or three times more, until no lead is left in it.",
          "sourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "confirm-lead",
      "name": "Confirm lead",
      "tube": "b",
      "reagentIds": [
        "acetic-acid-6m",
        "k2cro4-0.5m"
      ],
      "ruleIds": [
        "lead-chromate-forms"
      ],
      "substanceIds": [
        "lead-chromate",
        "chromate-ion",
        "lead-ion"
      ],
      "equilibriumIds": [],
      "equations": [
        {
          "value": "Pb²⁺(aq) + CrO₄²⁻(aq) → PbCrO₄(s)",
          "sourceId": "smc-group-1-lab",
          "alsoSourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "value": "A yellow solid is lead chromate, and it confirms lead. The chromate colours the liquid whether lead is there or not, so the colour of the liquid proves nothing: only a solid does. Centrifuging packs the solid down, which makes the two easy to tell apart.",
          "sourceId": "smc-group-1-lab",
          "alsoSourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab",
          "moreSourceIds": [
            "hope-q1-analysis"
          ]
        },
        {
          "value": "The acetic acid is there to keep the chromates of other metals from coming down as well. On a real bench the liquid is tested before it cools, because lead chloride can come back out of cooling water as a white solid. This lab takes the second tube to stay hot.",
          "sourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "separate",
      "name": "Part silver from mercury",
      "tube": "a",
      "reagentIds": [
        "nh3-6m"
      ],
      "ruleIds": [
        "silver-chloride-in-ammonia",
        "mercury-chloride-with-ammonia",
        "lead-chloride-with-ammonia"
      ],
      "substanceIds": [
        "silver-chloride",
        "silver-ammonia-ion",
        "mercury-i-chloride",
        "mercury-and-amidochloride",
        "lead-hydroxide"
      ],
      "equilibriumIds": [
        "silver-chloride",
        "silver-ammonia-ion"
      ],
      "equations": [
        {
          "value": "AgCl(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → [Ag(NH₃)₂]⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)",
          "sourceId": "birk-silver",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        },
        {
          "value": "Hg₂Cl₂(s) + 2NH₃(aq) → Hg(l) + HgNH₂Cl(s) + NH₄⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq)",
          "sourceId": "birk-mercury",
          "alsoSourceId": "smc-group-1-lab"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "value": "Silver chloride dissolves in ammonia. Each silver ion joins two ammonia molecules and becomes a colourless ion that stays in the liquid, so the silver is poured off with it.",
          "sourceId": "birk-silver",
          "alsoSourceId": "malik-varying-solubility"
        },
        {
          "value": "Mercury(I) chloride does not dissolve. Ammonia turns it into mercury metal, in specks so fine that they look black, and a white compound of mercury(II), mercury(II) amidochloride. Together they look grey to black, and that confirms mercury(I).",
          "sourceId": "birk-mercury",
          "alsoSourceId": "malik-group-1-ions",
          "moreSourceIds": [
            "smc-group-1-lab"
          ]
        },
        {
          "value": "Lead chloride left behind at step 2 does not dissolve in ammonia either. It stays as a white solid, which a university laboratory handout gives as lead hydroxide. A white solid left at this step is that lead: it is not silver, and it is not mercury(I).",
          "sourceId": "ntu-group-1-lab"
        }
